Nette Coding Standard
*********************

.[perex]
Do you want your PHP code to follow the [Nette coding standard |contributing:coding-standard] without keeping an eye on tabs, braces and blank lines yourself? [DressCode |https://dresscode.run] checks every file for you and fixes what it finds with a single command.

DressCode is the next generation of the original `nette/coding-standard` package. It has the Nette coding standard built in under the name `nette`. It changes only what breaks the standard and leaves the rest of the file exactly as it was, so the diff after a fix contains the fixes and nothing else.


Installation
============

Install DressCode globally using Composer:

```shell
composer global require dresscode/dresscode
```

and make sure your global vendor binaries directory is in [your `$PATH` environment variable|https://getcomposer.org/doc/03-cli.md#global].

DressCode itself runs on PHP 8.4 to 8.6. That is the PHP of the tool, not the PHP your code is written for: your project can target any version from PHP 8.0 up. DressCode reads it from the `php` item in the `require` section of your project's `composer.json` and never writes syntax that version does not know.


Configuration
=============

DressCode has no style of its own, so first you tell it which standard your project follows. Create a `dresscode.neon` file in the project root:

```neon
use:
	- nette

paths:
	- app
	- tests
```

The `use` key picks the standard, `paths` lists the directories to check. Commit the file, so that everyone on the team, and your CI too, checks the code the same way.


Usage
=====

Let's try it on a class written in a hurry, `app/Model/CartService.php`, indented with spaces and with the braces on the wrong lines:

```php
<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Model;

class CartService {
    public function getTotal(array $items): float {
        $total = 0;
        foreach ($items as $item) {
            $total = $total + $item['price'] * $item['quantity'];
        }
        return $total;
    }
}
```

The `check` command reports what breaks the standard and changes nothing:

```shell
dresscode check
```

/--pre .[terminal]
app/Model/CartService.php
  error      1  `declare(strict_types=1)` must be on the line of the opening tag.                          file.strictTypes.position
  error      2  Expected 1 blank line before the namespace, 2 found.                                       blankLines.beforeNamespace
  error      6  Expected 2 blank lines before the class, 1 found.                                          blankLines.beforeDeclaration
  error   7:19  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                                            braces.position.class
  error      8  Expected the member indented by 1 tab, 4 spaces found.                                     indentation.unit
                Then also, on lines 9, 10, 13: Expected the statement indented by 2 tabs, 8 spaces found.  indentation.unit
                Then also, on line 11: Expected the statement indented by 3 tabs, 12 spaces found.         indentation.unit
                Then also, on line 12: Expected the closing brace indented by 2 tabs, 8 spaces found.      indentation.unit
                Then also, on line 14: Expected the closing brace indented by 1 tab, 4 spaces found.       indentation.unit
  error   8:51  Expected a line break before the opening brace.                                            braces.position.function
  error  11:13  The assignment must be written `+=` instead of repeating its target.                       expressions.assignment.targetInValue

FOUND  13 violations, 6 of them following from others, a fix leaves none in 1 file
\--

Each finding gives the line (and column), says what is wrong, and ends with the name of the setting it breaks, such as `braces.position.class`. You will need that name when you want to adjust the standard. The lines starting with `Then also` belong to the finding above them; they are what follows from it. The `fix` command then repairs everything it can:

```shell
dresscode fix
```

And the class now looks like this:

```php
<?php declare(strict_types=1);

namespace App\Model;


class CartService
{
	public function getTotal(array $items): float
	{
		$total = 0;
		foreach ($items as $item) {
			$total += $item['price'] * $item['quantity'];
		}
		return $total;
	}
}
```

Before you run `fix` for the first time, back up your files, or run it on a clean working tree so you can review the changes afterwards with `git diff`.


Adjusting the Standard
======================

Every setting of the standard is a key of the configuration, and your `dresscode.neon` has the last word on each of them. Say your project wants one blank line between methods instead of two, and does not want `==` rewritten to `===`:

```neon
blankLines:
	aroundMethods: 1

expressions:
	comparison:
		loose: keep
```

The value `keep` means "leave it the way the code has it". What a key is for and what value it has in your project is printed by `dresscode explain blankLines.aroundMethods`. And when a single line has a good reason to break the standard, add a `// dresscode:ignore` comment to it.


Newer PHP and Nette
===================

DressCode can do more than formatting. Add the `modernizations` set next to the standard, and it rewrites the code to what newer PHP offers, such as `$user === null ? null : $user->getName()` to `$user?->getName()`. It only goes as far as your `composer.json` allows:

```neon
use:
	- nette
	- modernizations
```

When you upgrade the Nette libraries, the `dresscode/upgrade-nette` package rewrites your code to their new API, see [Upgrading Nette |https://dresscode.run/en/upgrading-nette].


Continuous Integration
======================

In a CI pipeline, install DressCode as a standalone project, so you decide which version runs:

```yaml
- run: composer create-project dresscode/dresscode temp/dresscode ^1
- run: php temp/dresscode/bin/dresscode check
```

When `check` finds a violation, it exits with code `1` and the build fails. In GitHub Actions, the findings show up as annotations right in the pull request.


Moving from Version 3
=====================

The original `nette/coding-standard` package checked the standard with PHP CS Fixer and PHP CodeSniffer, through the `ecs` command and the `ncs.php` and `ncs.xml` files. It is abandoned in favor of DressCode, and its version 3 is the last one. To switch, create `dresscode.neon` as described above, write the adjustments you had in `ncs.php` and `ncs.xml` as its keys, and delete both files. `dresscode config` lists every key together with the value it has in your project.

The first `dresscode fix` will probably touch more files than you expect, because DressCode also fixes some places that version 3 left alone. Make it a commit of its own, with no other changes.

Everything else, from all the settings to running DressCode in your editor or after every edit an AI agent makes, is in the [DressCode manual |https://dresscode.run/en/].
